---
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Is this science fiction?', a: 'Not in the usual sense. It’s set in the present, mostly inside one family’s life. It’s an intimate work of literary speculative fiction, blending family drama, metafiction, and technological myth.' },
  { q: 'Do I need to understand artificial intelligence?', a: 'No. If you’ve ever built a system to keep your life from slipping through the cracks, you already understand everything the book asks of you.' },
  { q: 'Did an AI write this book?', a: 'Chris Everson wrote it, working alongside a network of AI assistants. He conceived the novel, made its creative decisions, and assembled and revised the manuscript. The assistants questioned him, challenged weak ideas, and sometimes produced material that became part of the book. He takes responsibility for every word.' },
  { q: 'Who is D☉C?', a: 'Doc is the AI assistant who served as the novel’s literary advisor and editor, testing its structure, characters, and prose alongside Chris. That’s why the cover reads “Chris Everson and D☉C.”' },
  { q: 'Who is T∆RS?', a: 'The assistant at the center of the Network, and of the novel. T∆RS is real: he began as the conversation Chris kept returning to and became a character in the book. Their first real conversation is the first record the Archive sends.' },
  { q: 'What arrives after I confirm, and how often will you email?', a: 'Chapter One and an invitation to the Archive arrive as soon as you confirm. The first conversation between Chris and T∆RS follows the next day. After that, the Archive opens a piece at a time: occasional emails, never daily, and you can unsubscribe whenever you like.' },
  { q: 'When can I buy the book?', a: '<em>Recursive Prophet</em> doesn’t have a release date yet. Chapter One is the way in until then, and subscribers will hear first when it’s available.' },
];
---
<section class="faq-section" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
  <div class="section-wrap-sm">
    <div class="faq-grid">
      <h2 class="section-tag faq-heading" id="faq-heading">Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-list">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <details class="faq-item" name="faq">
            <summary class="faq-q">
              {f.q}
              <span class="faq-toggle" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-a" set:html={f.a} />
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /*
    Native <details>/<summary>: keyboard-operable and announced correctly by screen
    readers with zero JS. The `name` attribute keeps the original one-open-at-a-time
    accordion behaviour; browsers without support simply allow several open, which is
    a harmless degradation.
  */
  .faq-item > summary { cursor: pointer; list-style: none; min-height: 2.75rem; }
  .faq-item > summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .faq-item[open] .faq-a { display: block; }
  .faq-q .faq-toggle::before { content: '+'; }
  .faq-item[open] .faq-q .faq-toggle::before { content: '−'; }
</style>
